@page "/color"

<PageTitle>Color spaces · Bmotion</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Color spaces</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Interpolating a color means walking a straight line between two points - but <em>which</em>
        space you draw that line in decides what the midpoints look like. In sRGB the channels are
        mixed numerically, which is why two distant colors pass through a dark, muddy middle on the
        way. <strong>OKLab</strong> is built so that equal steps look like equal steps: the lightness
        travels evenly from one end to the other, so the same journey stays clean and bright the
        whole way across. Opposite hues still meet near grey in the middle - in any space a straight
        line between them crosses it - but a light grey, not a dark one.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> sRGB and OKLab side by side</h3>
    <p>
        sRGB is the default, matching Framer Motion. Opt into the perceptual space per transition
        with <code>colorSpace: BmColorSpace.Oklab</code>, or for a whole subtree with
        <code>&lt;BmotionConfig ColorSpace="BmColorSpace.Oklab"&gt;</code>. The pairs below run the
        same transition over the same 1.6&nbsp;s - watch the middle of each run, not the endpoints,
        which are identical by definition.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        @foreach (var pair in _pairs)
        {
            <div class="demo-card stack">
                <span class="demo-label">@pair.Label</span>
                <div class="swatch-pair">
                    <div class="swatch-cell">
                        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? pair.To : pair.From)" Transition="Bm.Tween(1.6)">
                            <div class="swatch" style="@($"background:{pair.FromCss}")" />
                        </Bmotion>
                        <span class="demo-label">sRGB</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="swatch-cell">
                        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? pair.To : pair.From)"
                                 Transition="Bm.Tween(1.6, colorSpace: BmColorSpace.Oklab)">
                            <div class="swatch" style="@($"background:{pair.FromCss}")" />
                        </Bmotion>
                        <span class="demo-label">OKLab</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">@(_on ? "Reverse" : "Cross-fade")</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_code" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> When it matters</h3>
    <p>
        Not every transition needs it. The two colors have to be far enough apart for the midpoint
        to be visible at all, so the difference is obvious on opposites - blue to yellow, red to
        green - and invisible between two neighbouring blues. The cost is a per-frame conversion, so
        the sensible default is what Bmotion does: sRGB unless you ask, and OKLab where the
        midpoints are actually on screen long enough to be seen.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><strong>Reach for OKLab</strong> when a slow transition crosses the color wheel, or when a hue animates through a keyframe palette.</li>
        <li><strong>Leave it at sRGB</strong> for short state changes, hover tints, and any pair of nearby colors.</li>
        <li><strong>Set it globally</strong> on <code>BmotionConfig</code> when a whole surface should behave consistently rather than deciding per animation.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private bool _on;

    /// <summary>One comparison: the two ends of the transition, plus the CSS the swatch starts in.</summary>
    private sealed record ColorPair(string Label, string FromCss, BmProps From, BmProps To);

    private static ColorPair Pair(string label, string from, string to)
        => new(label, from, Bm.To(backgroundColor: from), Bm.To(backgroundColor: to));

    private readonly ColorPair[] _pairs =
    [
        Pair("blue → yellow", "#0000FF", "#FFDD00"),
        Pair("red → green", "#E01B24", "#26A269"),
        Pair("magenta → teal", "#C61Ecb", "#12B8A6"),
    ];

    private const string _code = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Per-transition. sRGB is the default, matching Framer Motion. *@
        <Bmotion Animate='@(_on ? Bm.To(backgroundColor: "#FFDD00") : Bm.To(backgroundColor: "#0000FF"))'
                 Transition="Bm.Tween(1.6, colorSpace: BmColorSpace.Oklab)">
            <div class="swatch" style="background:#0000FF" />
        </Bmotion>
        <button @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">Cross-fade</button>

        @* …or once, for a whole subtree. *@
        <BmotionConfig ColorSpace="BmColorSpace.Oklab">
            ...
        </BmotionConfig>

        @code {
            private bool _on;
        }
        """;
}
